 if t==28:
  teach('6.2.3-02','Relative charge/mass in the school comparison are alpha +2/about 4, beta-minus −1/about 1/1840 and gamma 0/zero. For the usual comparable beam illustration, beta bends more strongly than alpha because its mass is much smaller; exact curvature also depends on energy.')
  teach('6.2.2-01','Medical exposures and fallout are additional human-related contributors to radiation exposure; their contribution depends on location and circumstances.')
  teach('6.2.1-01','An approved school source is kept in its specified shielded labelled container in locked storage, handled by the authorised teacher with a holder or tongs, kept directed away from people and returned promptly. Shielding must suit the radiation and source, not an arbitrary material.')
  extra('6.2.5-01','Coin model of half-life','Equipment: at least 100 identical coins, a tray and a results table. Count the initial coins. Shake and toss the whole remaining set; remove coins showing heads and record tails remaining. Repeat with the remaining coins for several rounds. Repeat the entire investigation with a fresh full set, keeping the removal rule and tossing method fixed. Plot mean survivors against round number. About half survive each round, so the model half-life is about one round; real runs fluctuate. No individual coin has a scheduled removal round. This models random decay and exponential decrease; it does not measure a radioactive material’s half-life in seconds. A six-sided die with one chosen face removed instead has survival probability 5/6 per round, so its half-life is longer than one round.')
